# {{APP_NAME}}

Go application generated from gobase.

- Go: 1.27.1 or newer
- HTTP engine: `{{ENGINE}}`
- Go module: `{{MODULE}}`
- Database: PostgreSQL 17
- Queue: Redis 7 and Asynq

The engine is recorded in `.gobase.json` for code generation. Editing this file does not migrate the application to another engine.

## Included Functionality

The application includes HTTP routes for register, login, refresh, logout, and the current user. It also includes article CRUD, profile management, media storage, translation, and background workers.

Email verification and password reset have use cases and token persistence. Their HTTP routes and queued email tasks still need to be connected. These flows and OpenTelemetry metrics export are deferred template features. OpenTelemetry initializes tracing. The application bootstrap closes cache and rate limiter Redis clients after HTTP shutdown. Non-root Docker execution remains planned foundation work.

## Run Locally

Install Go and Docker with Docker Compose. From this application's directory:

```bash
cp .env.example .env
cp config/config.example.yaml config/config.yaml
make docker-services
make run
```

The application applies migrations automatically in development. Start it before starting the worker in another terminal:

```bash
make run-worker
```

The `.env` file supplies Docker Compose variables. Local Go commands read `config/config.yaml` and exported environment variables. Update both configurations when changing database or Redis ports.

New projects use local storage. MinIO is optional. See [deployment](docs/deployment.md) to configure an S3 endpoint or enable the MinIO profile.

```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/healthz
curl http://localhost:8080/readyz
```

Swagger UI is available at `http://localhost:8080/swagger/`. Stop local Go processes with Ctrl+C. Stop the Docker services with `make docker-stop`.

`GET /v1/articles` accepts an omitted or empty `status`, or the exact values `draft` and `published`. Other values return HTTP 400 with `VALIDATION_ERROR` and field details. Malformed queries retain `INVALID_QUERY`. Pagination uses `page` and `limit` with the existing defaults and normalization.

## Run in Docker

After copying the configuration files, build and start the app, worker, and infrastructure:

```bash
make docker-dev-build
make docker-logs
make docker-stop
```

Docker Compose uses service names for database and Redis connections. Runtime ports inside the containers remain 5432, 6379, and 8080. Environment variables in `.env` select the host ports.

The app and worker share the `upload_data` Docker volume for local storage.

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    HTTP[Selected HTTP engine] --> Handler[Native handlers]
    Handler --> UseCase[Use cases]
    Worker[Asynq worker] --> Repo
    UseCase --> Repo[Repository interfaces]
    Repo --> Database[(PostgreSQL)]
    UseCase --> Queue[(Redis)]
    Queue --> Worker
```

Use cases, DTOs, entities, repositories, and workers contain the shared application behavior. HTTP handlers under `internal/handlers/http` use the selected engine's API. See [code patterns](docs/code-patterns.md) for handler signatures and response helpers.

## Add a Feature

Install the migration CLI if needed:

```bash
go install -tags postgres github.com/golang-migrate/migrate/v4/cmd/migrate@v4.19.1
make migrate-create name=create_orders
```

Edit the SQL migration, then generate its layers using the actual filename:

```bash
make gen-full MIGRATION=000012_create_orders.up.sql
make wire
make swag
make build
make check-all
```

Restarting the development app applies new migrations. Production deployments run migrations explicitly. See [deployment](docs/deployment.md).

## Verify

```bash
make test
make check-all
```

Install golangci-lint v2.14.0 or a newer version compatible with Go 1.27 for the full lint check. Integration tests need a running application, PostgreSQL, and Redis:

```bash
APP_HOST=localhost APP_PORT=8080 make test-integration
```

Integration tests create application records. Use a separate development database when running them.

